// JupyterLab keyboard-shortcut overrides for presenting.
//
// Problem: Jupyter's default Shift+Enter is `notebook:run-cell-and-select-next`.
// The "select next" half is what moves you on -- when the next cell belongs to
// the next slide, RISE follows the selection and the deck jumps forward the
// instant the cell finishes. Mid-lecture that means you run a demo and never
// see its output.
//
// Fix: disable that binding and point Shift+Enter at `notebook:run-cell`, which
// runs the cell and leaves the selection (and therefore the slide) alone.
// Advance deliberately with Space / Right / Down instead.
//
// Ctrl+Enter and Cmd+Enter are already bound to `notebook:run-cell` by default,
// so they behave this way with or without this file.
//
// Installed by present.sh (into its isolated config dir) and by
// .devcontainer/setup.sh (into the container's ~/.jupyter). Delete either copy
// to get stock Shift+Enter behaviour back.
//
// Second problem, same cause: Left/Right arrows skipped whole slides.
//
// JupyterLab binds ArrowRight to `notebook:move-cursor-heading-below-or-expand`
// (and ArrowLeft to the -above- variant) in command mode. Those jump the active
// cell to the next/previous *markdown heading*, and RISE follows the active
// cell to whichever slide it lives on. Content slides start with a `##`
// heading, but the exercise cards and live-example banners are pure HTML with no
// heading -- so the arrows leapt straight over them. Measured on Lecture 4:
// one keypress went from slide 20 to 22, skipping the "Fruits Beginning with a
// Vowel" exercise entirely. `Space` was unaffected because it never reaches
// Lumino's keymap.
//
// Disabling both lets reveal.js handle the arrows natively, so they step one
// slide at a time like Space does.
//
// Third problem, same cause: typing into an `input()` prompt loses characters.
//
// CS 1 is full of `input()` -- Lecture 5's "A Simple While Loop" asks the class
// to type "yes" to end the loop -- and the box would only ever receive "ye".
//
// RISE contributes its own shortcuts (schemas/jupyterlab-rise/plugin.json), and
// unlike JupyterLab's own single-letter notebook shortcuts, their selector is
//
//     .lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode
//
// with NO `:not(.jp-mod-readWrite)` guard. While a stdin prompt is focused the
// notebook node carries BOTH `jp-mod-commandMode` and `jp-mod-readWrite`, so
// JupyterLab's own letter shortcuts (A B C J K M R V X Y Z, 1-6) correctly stand
// down -- which is why "y" and "e" arrive -- but every RISE letter matches and
// Lumino claims it at document capture, calling preventDefault() and
// stopPropagation() before the character can reach the input. Measured: the
// keydown is un-prevented at window-capture and already prevented at
// document-capture, and focus drops from the input to the cell.
//
// So each of these letters is unusable in any typed answer:
//
//     S  RISE:chalkboard-colorNext          T  RISE:notes-open
//     W  RISE:toggleOverview                Q  RISE:chalkboard-colorPrev
//     F  RISE:fullscreenHelp
//
// plus the punctuation ones, which matter because CS 1 asks for negative and
// comma-grouped numbers: minus, =, [, ], \, and "," .
//
// `T` was also the mystery "a reveal.js window just popped up mid-lecture":
// RISE:notes-open calls window.open("about:blank", "reveal.js - Notes"), and on
// every later press it just re-focuses that window, so it keeps stealing the
// screen.
//
// Disabling them costs almost nothing here: `enable_chalkboard` is false (the
// RISE default, and this repo does not turn it on), so the five chalkboard
// bindings are dead keys that only eat typing. The overview grid is still
// reachable from RISE's own on-screen buttons, and Escape/`?` still work.
//
// Deliberately KEPT: Alt+R (start/stop), Shift+Enter (run), Shift+I/B/G (set
// slide type), Home/End, and `?` (help) -- none is a letter a student types
// into a prompt.
//
// Why the letters had to go, concretely: Lectures 2, 4, 7 and 12 ask for a
// NAME, a STRING and a CITY NAME ("What is your name?", "Please enter a
// string:", "Enter a city name"). Any of s/t/w/q/f is ordinary in an answer --
// "Stewart", "Seattle", "Quinn" -- so those keys have to reach the box. The
// `minus` binding matters for the same reason: Lecture 4 asks for a Fahrenheit
// temperature, which can be negative.
//
// REBOUND rather than lost. Two of the disabled commands are worth keeping for
// a presenter, so they move to Alt combinations, which no typed answer can
// produce. Note that reveal.js's own overview keys are NOT a fallback: RISE
// passes reveal a `keyboard` config that nulls keyCode 27 (Escape) and 79 (O)
// as well as 87 (W), so `RISE:toggleOverview` was the only way in and
// disabling it bare left no way at all.
//
//     Alt+W   slide overview       (was W)
//     Alt+T   speaker-notes window (was T)
//     Alt+,   show/hide RISE's own on-screen buttons (was ,)
//
// The rest are chalkboard commands, and `enable_chalkboard` is false, so there
// is nothing behind them to reach.
{
  "shortcuts": [
    {
      "command": "notebook:move-cursor-heading-below-or-expand",
      "keys": ["ArrowRight"],
      "selector": ".jp-Notebook.jp-mod-commandMode:not(.jp-mod-readWrite) :focus",
      "disabled": true
    },
    {
      "command": "notebook:move-cursor-heading-above-or-collapse",
      "keys": ["ArrowLeft"],
      "selector": ".jp-Notebook.jp-mod-commandMode:not(.jp-mod-readWrite) :focus",
      "disabled": true
    },
    {
      "command": "notebook:run-cell-and-select-next",
      "keys": ["Shift Enter"],
      "selector": ".jp-Notebook.jp-mod-editMode",
      "disabled": true
    },
    {
      "command": "notebook:run-cell",
      "keys": ["Shift Enter"],
      "selector": ".jp-Notebook.jp-mod-editMode"
    },
    {
      "command": "notebook:run-cell",
      "keys": ["Shift Enter"],
      "selector": ".jp-Notebook.jp-mod-commandMode:not(.jp-mod-readWrite) :focus"
    },

    { "command": "RISE:chalkboard-colorNext",
      "keys": ["S"], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode",
      "disabled": true },
    { "command": "RISE:notes-open",
      "keys": ["T"], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode",
      "disabled": true },
    { "command": "RISE:toggleOverview",
      "keys": ["W"], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode",
      "disabled": true },
    { "command": "RISE:chalkboard-colorPrev",
      "keys": ["Q"], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode",
      "disabled": true },
    { "command": "RISE:fullscreenHelp",
      "keys": ["F"], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-Notebook:focus",
      "disabled": true },
    { "command": "RISE:chalkboard-clear",
      "keys": ["minus"], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode",
      "disabled": true },
    { "command": "RISE:chalkboard-reset",
      "keys": ["="], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode",
      "disabled": true },
    { "command": "RISE:chalkboard-toggleChalkboard",
      "keys": ["["], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode",
      "disabled": true },
    { "command": "RISE:chalkboard-toggleNotesCanvas",
      "keys": ["]"], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode",
      "disabled": true },
    { "command": "RISE:chalkboard-download",
      "keys": ["\\"], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode",
      "disabled": true },
    { "command": "RISE:toggleAllRiseButtons",
      "keys": [","], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode",
      "disabled": true },

    { "command": "RISE:toggleOverview",
      "keys": ["Alt W"], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode" },
    { "command": "RISE:notes-open",
      "keys": ["Alt T"], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode" },
    { "command": "RISE:toggleAllRiseButtons",
      "keys": ["Alt ,"], "selector": ".lm-Widget.reveal .jp-mod-commandMode" }
  ]
}
